{"type":"message","id":"636d9b7b","parentId":"d9309c9f","timestamp":"2026-04-15T11:51:55.939Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I see that OpenClaw doesn't have a built-in `/aigit` command for switching providers. Custom slash commands like `/aigit` would need to be implemented differently.\n\nLooking at the schema, I don't see a dedicated slash command registry. The `/aigit` command would need to be handled by a custom plugin or as a text command.\n\nFor the model switching, OpenClaw uses `agents.defaults.model.primary` with format `provider/modelId`. To switch models, the user can use `/model gitcode/qwen3.5-397b-a17b` or I could create a custom command.\n\nActually, looking at the config, there's no built-in way to add custom slash commands via config alone. \n\nI think the cleanest approach is to handle `/aigit` as a text command that calls a tool to update the config at runtime, or use a per-session model override mechanism. Let me look into whether OpenClaw supports runtime model switching through text commands or if I need to go with a plugin-based solution.\n\nFor the GitCode provider integration, I'm setting up the model configuration with the base URL and API key, then enabling the `/aigit` command as a text command that invokes a tool to handle the model switching. Setting up the model configuration with image support and API parameters like temperature and top_p, then handling model switching through the `/aigit` command which patches the agent config to toggle between the custom Qwen model and the default Coze provider. I need to clarify whether OpenClaw supports direct config patching via `/config` commands or if I'd need to implement a custom skill to handle model switching, and I'm also reconsidering whether that SecretRef value is actually correct for this setup. I need to understand the actual config structure better—specifically whether `providers` is a map or array and how model references work. Looking at the schema hint, it seems `models.mode` controls whether custom providers merge with or replace the default ones, so I should use merge mode and add gitcode as a new provider. My plan is to add the provider config, set it as primary, and then figure out the best approach for the `/aigit` command.\n\nFor that command, I'm realizing a native command approach might be complex, so instead I'll leverage the `/config` command to handle the model switching directly—users can toggle between models with simple commands like `/config agents.defaults.model.primary=gitcode/qwen3-5-397b-a17b` and restart, or I could create a helper skill that wraps this functionality more cleanly.\n\nNow I'm configuring the provider settings with the Qwen3.5-397B model that supports both text and image inputs, along with its cost structure and context window specifications.\n\nConfiguring model switching commands...\n\nI need to figure out the right config path for the primary model—whether it's `agents.defaults.model` or `agents.defaults.model.primary`. Looking at the existing config with `\"primary\": \"coze/auto\"`, it seems like `primary` might be a property of `agents.defaults.model` rather than a separate key, so the full config path would be `agents.defaults.model.primary`.\n\nFor the patch operation, I need to structure it as a nested object under `agents.defaults.model.primary`. Since OpenClaw uses dot-notation paths internally, when patching nested objects, I should format it as `agents.defaults.model.primary` with the value being just the string `\"gitcode/qwen3.5-397b-a17b\"`.\n\nBut I'm realizing the distinction between `config.patch` (which does a deep merge) and `config.apply` (which replaces the entire config) — patch will only update the fields I specify without affecting other parts of the config.\n\nNow I'm trying to figure out whether model references in OpenClaw use provider/modelId format like `coze/auto`, and what the internal model ID format looks like for the qwen3.5 model.\n\nI'm planning to configure the gitcode provider with the Qwen3.5-397B-A17B model, set it as the primary model, and then use config.patch to handle the model switching.\n\nWriting provider configuration...","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_mx3zs5j6bt8g_1","name":"gateway","arguments":{"action":"config.schema.lookup","path":"agents.defaults.model"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"coze","model":"auto","usage":{"input":3154,"output":1746,"cacheRead":29563,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":34463,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1776253890052}}
